Here is a basic implementation of an autocomplete component in React without relying on other third-party packages:

```jsx
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

const AutoComplete = ({ suggestions, renderSuggestion }) => {
  const [inputValue, setInputValue] = useState('');
  const [suggestionsList, setSuggestionsList] = useState([]);
  const [activeSuggestionIndex, setActiveSuggestionIndex] = useState(-1);

  useEffect(() => {
    const handleInputChange = (event) => {
      const value = event.target.value;
      setInputValue(value);
      const suggestionsList = suggestions.filter((suggestion) =>
        suggestion.toLowerCase().includes(value.toLowerCase())
      );
      setSuggestionsList(suggestionsList);
    };

    document.addEventListener('input', handleInputChange);

    return () => {
      document.removeEventListener('input', handleInputChange);
    };
  }, [suggestions]);

  const handleClick = (suggestion) => {
    setInputValue(suggestion);
    setActiveSuggestionIndex(-1);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        type="text"
        value={inputValue}
        onChange={(event) => setInputValue(event.target.value)}
        onFocus={() => setActiveSuggestionIndex(-1)}
        onBlur={() => setActiveSuggestionIndex(-1)}
        onKeyUp={(event) => {
          if (event.key === 'ArrowDown') {
            setActiveSuggestionIndex((activeSuggestionIndex + 1) % suggestionsList.length);
          } else if (event.key === 'ArrowUp') {
            setActiveSuggestionIndex(Math.max(0, activeSuggestionIndex - 1));
          } else if (event.key === 'Enter') {
            if (activeSuggestionIndex >= 0) {
              handleClick(suggestionsList[activeSuggestionIndex]);
            }
          }
        }}
      />
      {activeSuggestionIndex >= 0 && (
        <ul>
          {suggestionsList.map((suggestion, index) => (
            <li
              key={suggestion}
              className={index === activeSuggestionIndex ? 'active' : ''}
              onClick={() => handleClick(suggestion)}
            >
              {renderSuggestion(suggestion)}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  );
};

export default AutoComplete;
```

In this implementation, we're using the React state to store the current input value and the list of suggestions. We're also using a state variable to track the currently active suggestion.

When the user types something into the input field, we filter the list of suggestions to show only the ones that match the user's input. We also update the state to store the filtered list of suggestions.

When the user clicks on an arrow key, we update the activeSuggestionIndex to show the next or previous suggestion in the list. When the user clicks on Enter, we select the currently active suggestion.

Finally, we render the input field and the list of suggestions. We use a separate component for rendering the suggestion (renderSuggestion), which you can replace with your own logic for rendering the suggestions.

